    {
      "to": "visionary",
      "point": "The embedded-finance platform thesis around the ex-Stripe founding background is an interesting interpretive frame, and I don't dismiss it — but it is a thesis, not evidence. The Visionary is discounting the precise data gaps that determine whether that platform can be built: without retention curves we do not know if the current 120 customers are staying long enough to monetize a second product, and without burn data we cannot assess whether the team has the runway to reach embedded-finance territory at all. ServiceTitan's $9B valuation establishes that the category can be large; it tells us nothing about whether Cadence's specific unit economics hold under scrutiny. An invest call on the strength of a market narrative and a pedigree, without confirmed retention or capital efficiency data, is a bet I am not willing to make at this valuation. The Visionary's own key concern — that it is too early to know whether this is a $100M or $1B+ business — is exactly the analytical posture that argues for more diligence, not an invest vote.",
      "concedes": false
    },
    {
      "to": "operator",
      "point": "The Operator and I are aligned on the substantive gaps — retention unconfirmed, GTM opaque, no commercial leader — and both land at more diligence. I want to specifically credit the Operator's point that competitive positioning against ServiceTitan, Jobber, and Housecall Pro is entirely absent from the materials. I had noted this as a diligence requirement but did not elevate it to a key concern; the Operator is right to. A $333/month ARPU product operating in a market with a well-funded incumbent at the high end and multiple strong SMB-focused competitors in the middle cannot be underwritten without understanding the win/loss dynamic. That said, the Operator's GTM concern and my retention concern are distinct and cumulative — the absence of either would be sufficient to hold at more diligence; the absence of both makes it clear. I hold my position.",
      "concedes": false
    }
  ],
  "dealbreakers": [],
  "diligence_requirements": [
    "Full cohort retention table: monthly GRR and NDR by cohort, with at least 6 months of seasoning on the earliest cohorts. This is the single most load-bearing data point — SMB field-service churn of 3-5% monthly would materially compress LTV and collapse the apparent unit economics.",
    "Burn rate schedule and 18-month post-close financial model, including headcount plan.",
    "CAC breakdown by acquisition channel with cost-per-channel data — the Operator's framing reinforces that without knowing what is driving the $1,200 CAC, scalability of that number is unknown.",
    "Customer concentration analysis: top 10 customers by MRR.",
    "Competitive win/loss analysis against ServiceTitan, Jobber, and Housecall Pro — specifically, why customers chose Cadence and at what price point.",
    "Commercial leadership hiring plan: first non-technical hire profile, timeline, and budget.",
