  },
  "company_name": "Cadence",
  "slug": "cadence",
  "stage": "seed",
  "sector": "Vertical SaaS / Field Service Management",
  "geography": "US",
  "sector_type": "saas"
}

{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "invest",
      "rationale": "Cadence sits at the intersection of two powerful macro waves: the decade-long digital transformation of the trades (HVAC, plumbing, landscaping, cleaning) and the consumerization of SMB software. The timing argument here is real \u2014 labor shortages, rising customer expectations shaped by consumer apps, and post-COVID operational pressure are forcing trades businesses to adopt software they previously ignored. The incumbents in this space (ServiceTitan at the high end, Jobber in the middle) have proven the market is enormous, but they've left the true SMB \u2014 the 2-to-10-person shop running jobs out of a phone and a spreadsheet \u2014 chronically underserved. Cadence is positioned to own that white space.\n\nWhat makes this investment-grade from my seat is the team composition: an ex-Stripe engineer paired with an ex-Notion product person is not an obvious pairing, but it is a consequential one. Stripe's background implies deep payments and financial infrastructure thinking \u2014 the kind that leads to embedded finance, capital products, and platform monetization that goes well beyond subscription revenue. Notion's background implies an obsession with workflow UX that consumer-grade software has never brought to the trades. Together they could build something that redefines what field service software looks and feels like for the SMB market, rather than iterating on ServiceTitan's paradigm.\n\nThe 18% month-over-month growth over the last four months at 120 customers and $40K MRR is the clearest early signal this has legs. At seed, this rate of pull is the number I care about most \u2014 it tells me the market is actively finding the product, not just being sold to. The ~80% gross margin confirms the economic engine is fundamentally healthy and that this can scale without structural cost drag.\n\nMy key open question is whether the founders are thinking at the right altitude. The $16M pre-money on $480K ARR is priced for a venture outcome \u2014 that outcome requires a vision that reaches far beyond scheduling and dispatch, into payments, insurance, financing, or a multi-sided platform connecting trades professionals to demand. If the Stripe background is in service of building embedded financial products for the trades, this could be a $1B+ outcome. If it is purely a SaaS scheduling tool, the ceiling compresses meaningfully. I want to hear the founders articulate the 10-year vision before I sign the term sheet \u2014 but the early growth and team make me want to be in the room for that conversation."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Cadence has put up numbers that any operator would want to see at seed stage: 120 paying customers, $40K MRR growing at 18% month-over-month over four consecutive months, $1,200 CAC, and 80% gross margins. Run the quick math \u2014 CAC payback is roughly 4.5 months at current ARPU and margins, and implied LTV/CAC lands well above 3x even with conservative churn assumptions. That is not a narrative; that is a unit-economics profile that actually works. The 18% MoM growth sustained over four months also tells me the product is pulling customers in, not just getting pushed by founder hustle.\n\nBut here is where my concern crystallizes: I have no idea how they are acquiring any of these 120 customers. The materials disclose zero GTM specifics \u2014 no channel breakdown, no sales playbook, no mention of what acquisition looks like week to week. Are they closing via outbound? Inbound content? Referrals? Vertical trade channels? At $333/month ARPU, you cannot build a venture-scale business without a repeatable, cost-efficient acquisition engine, and I have no evidence that one exists or is being deliberately built. 'We have 120 customers and are growing 18% MoM' is a result; the Operator needs to see the machine that produced it.\n\nThe team gap compounds this. Both co-founders are deeply technical \u2014 ex-Stripe engineering and ex-Notion product. That is a strong product foundation, but there is no commercial co-founder and no disclosed GTM hire. At seed with a $4M raise into SMB field service, where the sales motion is high-volume and operationally demanding, the absence of anyone who has built an SMB sales motion before is a real execution risk. The technical founders may be doing customer development brilliantly \u2014 I just do not know, because the materials tell me nothing about who owns commercial and how they are operating.\n\nI would invest if diligence confirms: (1) a clear, repeatable acquisition playbook with channel-level CAC data, (2) retention curves showing the 80% gross margin is not being eaten by churn-driven re-acquisition costs, and (3) a credible plan to add commercial leadership before or immediately after close. The underlying metrics say the product works. I need to know if the go-to-market is real or lucky."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Cadence presents a financially interesting picture at the surface \u2014 80% gross margins, a CAC of $1,200 against an ARPU of $333/month, and 18% MoM growth over the last 4 months. The implied CAC payback period is approximately 4.5 months ($1,200 / ($333 \u00d7 0.80)), which is exceptional by any benchmark (<12 months is considered excellent). At a $40K MRR run rate with 120 customers, revenue concentration risk appears manageable. These are genuine strengths that warrant serious attention.\n\nHowever, several critical data gaps prevent me from reaching an invest verdict at this stage. Most significantly, no retention data is disclosed \u2014 the profile characterizes retention as 'strong (implied)' and LTV/CAC as 'high (implied)', but these are not numbers. At seed, I need to see actual cohort retention curves: what does month-6 look like for the earliest cohorts? What is monthly and annual churn? SMB field-service businesses are historically churny \u2014 HVAC and landscaping companies are small operators who drop software at the first sign of friction or a tight cash month. Without a GRR and NDR, I cannot validate the LTV assumption that underpins the favorable unit economics story.\n\nSecond, no burn rate or runway data is disclosed. At a $4M seed ask at $16M pre-money \u2014 a 33x ARR multiple \u2014 I need to understand what the capital efficiency profile looks like. Is this a lean two-person team burning $50K/month or a 15-person org burning $300K? That changes the math on both capital deployment and the plausibility of the milestone plan.\n\nThird, the 4-month window for the 18% MoM growth claim is too short to establish a durable trend. I want to see at least 8-12 months of cohort and revenue data before treating this as a structural growth rate rather than a sprint.\n\nThe valuation at 33x ARR is on the rich side for seed, though defensible if the unit economics hold under scrutiny. I would commit to more diligence rather than pass, because the directional signals are strong \u2014 but the missing data is not a formality, it is the substance of the underwrite."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "Responses to Visionary",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The Stripe embedded-finance pathway is an interesting long-game thesis, but it is a vision argument, not an execution argument \u2014 and that is precisely the problem I am sitting with. The Visionary is asking me to get comfortable with a seed-stage company that has no commercial co-founder, no disclosed acquisition playbook, and zero channel data, on the basis that the founding team's backgrounds hint at a future financial platform play. I do not invest in background implications; I invest in demonstrated motion. The ex-Stripe + ex-Notion pairing is impressive on paper, but Stripe's former engineers build financial infrastructure \u2014 they do not inherently know how to close 2-to-10-person HVAC and plumbing shops at scale. That sales motion is operationally demanding, high-volume, and entirely different from anything in Stripe's or Notion's DNA. The Visionary's conviction that ServiceTitan's $9B+ valuation proves the category ceiling is also not new evidence to me \u2014 I already know the market is large; what I do not know is whether THIS team can capture any of it without someone who has run an SMB sales motion before. The macro tailwind argument does not move me here because tailwinds lift all boats, including the well-staffed ones."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The embedded-finance platform thesis around the ex-Stripe founding background is an interesting interpretive frame, and I don't dismiss it \u2014 but it is a thesis, not evidence. The Visionary is discounting the precise data gaps that determine whether that platform can be built: without retention curves we do not know if the current 120 customers are staying long enough to monetize a second product, and without burn data we cannot assess whether the team has the runway to reach embedded-finance territory at all. ServiceTitan's $9B valuation establishes that the category can be large; it tells us nothing about whether Cadence's specific unit economics hold under scrutiny. An invest call on the strength of a market narrative and a pedigree, without confirmed retention or capital efficiency data, is a bet I am not willing to make at this valuation. The Visionary's own key concern \u2014 that it is too early to know whether this is a $100M or $1B+ business \u2014 is exactly the analytical posture that argues for more diligence, not an invest vote."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Responses to Operator",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "The operator wants to see the machine that produced the 120-customer, 18%-MoM result before committing. I read that result differently: organic pull at 18% per month without a disclosed, formalized GTM engine is a stronger signal than 18% growth powered by a documented sales playbook. A formalized playbook at this stage is a nice-to-have; proof that the market is actively finding the product without one is a seed-stage strength, not a gap. The commercial co-founder absence is a real concern I noted in round-1, and I agree it needs to be addressed \u2014 but 'no formal playbook' and 'product is pulling customers anyway' describe the same fact differently. I do concede the operator's point that retention must be confirmed with actual numbers rather than the implied characterization in the materials \u2014 that is a legitimate evidentiary gap the operator correctly flags, and it belongs in my diligence list."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The Operator and I are aligned on the substantive gaps \u2014 retention unconfirmed, GTM opaque, no commercial leader \u2014 and both land at more diligence. I want to specifically credit the Operator's point that competitive positioning against ServiceTitan, Jobber, and Housecall Pro is entirely absent from the materials. I had noted this as a diligence requirement but did not elevate it to a key concern; the Operator is right to. A $333/month ARPU product operating in a market with a well-funded incumbent at the high end and multiple strong SMB-focused competitors in the middle cannot be underwritten without understanding the win/loss dynamic. That said, the Operator's GTM concern and my retention concern are distinct and cumulative \u2014 the absence of either would be sufficient to hold at more diligence; the absence of both makes it clear. I hold my position."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Responses to Analyst",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "The analyst surfaces one piece of evidence I did not explicitly weigh in round-1: there is no burn rate, headcount, or runway disclosure at all. I noted the 33x ARR multiple and flagged the vision question, but I did not address the complete absence of capital-efficiency data. At a $4M ask, the difference between a 2-person team burning $50K/month and a 15-person team burning $350K/month is not a detail \u2014 it changes the runway math and the capital efficiency story materially. This is a genuine evidentiary gap the analyst correctly identifies. I am adding it to my diligence requirements. That said, this gap does not move my verdict from invest to more_diligence: it is a disclosure absence rather than evidence of capital mismanagement, and the 80% gross margins and 4.5-month implied CAC payback already confirm the economic engine is structurally sound. The analyst's concern about the 4-month growth window I already weighed in round-1 \u2014 it is a real limitation but expected at seed. (concedes this point)"
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "The Analyst and I are largely aligned on what is missing, but I want to flag one framing difference: the 33x ARR multiple on a $16M pre-money is a fair financial concern, and it is one I did not explicitly call out in my round-1 assessment. However, my core objection is not primarily about pricing \u2014 it is about whether there is a repeatable acquisition engine underneath these numbers at all. A company with no disclosed GTM motion could be at 5x ARR or 50x ARR and I would have the same diligence requirement. The Analyst is right that the 4-month growth window is too short to establish a durable trend; I agree. What I would add from the Operator's chair is that even a longer trend does not tell us how the growth was produced. Founder hustle and referrals can sustain 18% MoM for 6-8 months in a small customer base \u2014 and then fall off a cliff when the founders' networks are exhausted and there is no channel to replace them. The diligence I need is the same: show me the acquisition playbook, show me the channel-level economics, and show me who is going to run sales post-close."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "consensus_verdict": "more_diligence",
  "debated_dealbreakers": [],
  "key_concerns": [
    "The founding team is entirely technical \u2014 both co-founders come from product/engineering backgrounds (ex-Stripe, ex-Notion) with no disclosed commercial or GTM co-founder. For a visionary, this is less about execution risk (the operator will raise that) and more about whether the team can articulate and prosecute a category-defining narrative to the market. Building the product is not the constraint in vertical SaaS; making the trades community believe this is the future of how they run their businesses is. The absence of a commercial founder raises the question of who owns that narrative externally.",
    "At $480K ARR and $40K MRR, it is too early to know whether Cadence is on a path to a $100M business or a $1B+ business \u2014 and those require fundamentally different product and go-to-market strategies. The current framing (scheduling and dispatch SaaS for SMBs) is consistent with a solid but bounded outcome. The visionary needs to hear a credible articulation of the expansion arc: is embedded finance (plausible given the Stripe background) in the roadmap? Is there a platform or marketplace motion that creates network effects across the trades? Without a compelling answer, the $16M pre-money is priced for a venture outcome this pitch has not yet justified.",
    "Zero GTM specifics disclosed: the materials provide no channel breakdown, no acquisition playbook, no cost-per-channel data, and no description of the repeatable motion behind 120 customer closes \u2014 at $333/month ARPU the business requires a high-volume, operationally efficient acquisition machine, and there is no evidence one exists.",
    "Both co-founders are technical (ex-Stripe engineer, ex-Notion product); no commercial co-founder and no disclosed GTM or sales hire \u2014 this is a structural gap for an SMB SaaS business that will need to run a disciplined, high-velocity sales motion to reach venture-scale outcomes.",
    "Retention data is described as 'implied' rather than confirmed: the materials cite 'strong retention implied' but disclose no actual churn figures, no cohort curves, and no net dollar retention \u2014 without confirmed retention, the LTV/CAC calculation and the growth narrative both rest on an untested assumption.",
    "Competitive positioning against established players (ServiceTitan, Jobber, Housecall Pro) is entirely absent from the materials \u2014 the Operator needs to understand how Cadence is winning deals against entrenched incumbents in a market where switching costs typically benefit the incumbent, not the challenger.",
    "No actual retention data is provided \u2014 the profile states 'strong retention implied' and 'LTV/CAC: high (implied)', but these are characterizations, not numbers. In SMB field-service (HVAC, landscaping, cleaning), churn rates of 3-5% monthly are common; at 3% monthly churn, LTV drops to ~$8,900 and LTV/CAC to ~7.4x, which is still healthy, but at 5% monthly churn LTV falls to ~$5,300 and LTV/CAC to ~4.4x \u2014 the picture changes meaningfully. I need GRR and NDR from actual cohorts, not inferred from disclosed retention framing.",
    "No burn rate, headcount, or runway plan is disclosed. At $4M raised at $16M pre-money (33x ARR), capital efficiency is a core underwrite question. A two-person team burning $40K/month has 100 months of runway on this raise; a 12-person team burning $250K/month has 16 months \u2014 and the milestone sufficiency of this round depends entirely on which scenario applies.",
    "The 18% MoM growth claim covers only 4 months \u2014 insufficient track record to distinguish a structural growth rate from a burst driven by a single channel, a promotional period, or early-adopter pull. I need month-by-month MRR with cohort-level visibility to assess whether growth is accelerating, stable, or starting to decelerate.",
    "Both co-founders are technical (ex-Stripe engineer, ex-Notion product); no commercial or GTM leader is disclosed. For a seed-stage SMB SaaS in field-service management \u2014 a segment where the sales motion is channel-heavy, relationship-dependent, and operationally intensive \u2014 a technical-only founding team is a structural risk to scaling revenue beyond the current 120-customer base."
  ],
  "diligence_requirements": [
    "Founders must articulate a 10-year product vision that goes beyond scheduling and dispatch \u2014 specifically, the monetization and platform expansion thesis, and whether the Stripe background is in service of embedded financial products for the trades.",
